   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to including the Rondout Creek in the definition of \"inland waterways\" for the purposes of waterfront revitalization",
    "basePrintNo": "S7956",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7956-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §911, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7956\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to including the\nRondout Creek in the definition of \"inland waterways\" for the purposes\nof waterfront revitalization\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo designate the Rondout Creek as an Inland Waterway for purposes of\nthe State's Coastal and Inland waterways Program.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nThe bill amends Subdivision 4 of Section 911 of the Executive law by\nexpanding the definition of Inland Waterways to include the Rondout\nCreek.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nDesignation as an \"inland waterway\" makes the local communities along\nsuch waterway eligible to participate in New York's Coastal and Inland\nWaterways Program. The State offers a grant program, funded through\nthe Environmental Protection Fund, which assists communities in\ndeveloping and implementing local waterfront revitalization programs.\nThese initiatives can be valuable in revitalizing the area and in\nfostering the beneficial use of waterfront.\n\nIn recent years, several waterways have been added to the definition,\nreflecting the interest in, and benefits of this program. This\nlegislation would amend the definition to also include the Rondout\nCreek, located in Ulster and Sullivan counties.\n\nHistorically, the Rondout Creek served as an important waterway and\nconnector to the Delaware and Hudson Canal in the movement of\nbluestone, cement, coal and other natural resources that directly led\nto the economic boon and development of the greater Kingston and\nRondout areas in the early to mid-1800s. A young Sojourner Truth also\nnotably traversed the Creek on a regular basis as a child, traveling\nfrom Kingston to Port-Ewen using a chain driven Skillypot ferry that\nwas installed by Dutch settlers in the late 17\" century. Today, the\nCreek maintains its purpose as a vital outlet to the Hudson River,\nprovides recreational opportunities for fishing, swimming and boating\nand carries fresh headwaters from the Catskill Mountains into the\nRondout Reservoir which serves a significant portion of New York\nCity's drinking water needs. Furthermore, the tidal portion of the\nRondout Creek serves as a prime spawning area for migratory fish and\nan important overwintering area for the Creek's bass population.\nDesignating the Rondout Creek as an \"inland waterway\" will afford\nadjacent communities access to funding that will help them enhance and\nbeautify their waterfront areas. Improving upon these scenic and\npurposeful creekfront areas will also draw in visitors whose patronage\nat local businesses will provide a boon to the regional economy.\nLastly, tapping into New York's Coastal and Inland Waterways Program\nwill allow communities still recovering from Superstorms Sandy and Lee\nto complete ongoing restoration and revitalization projects and engage\nin storm hardening initiatives that will better protect their\nwaterfront areas from flooding caused by future natural disasters.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
